hi there steve kaufman here sitting in  my yard here in palm springs and again  talking about language learning  and i'm going to follow up on my  discussion last week about input and  about motivation and i'm going to make  some reference to  taoism or taoism and zhuangza and  certain  sort of aspects of eastern philosophy  and how i feel that it relates to  acquiring a skill like a language  so  i mentioned last time that uh stephen  krashen had said that motivation is not  the way we get people to learn because a  majority of  children in school are not particularly  motivated to learn  and we have to kind of grab them with  interesting compelling content and of  course that is true  however  i find as a learner  that i can vary the interesting and i  need to have that interesting compelling  content  i did an interview for example this  morning in russian  [Music]  i just said that i gave an interview in  russian and everything was fairly well  understood between what my russian  counterpart had to say which i  understood entirely and of what i had to  say  all the while aware of my mistakes but  it doesn't matter  so  um  the point i wanted to make was with the  russian is that my motivation to learn  russian was the desire and this came up  in our discussion was the desire to read  the works of russian literature in the  original  and i was asked like why was this a  motivation and i said well  because the sense of reading these works  in the original  a gives you a sense of achievement and  gives you a sense of something special  you're in touch with the original it's a  wonderful feeling so that was my  motivation not everyone is motivated  that way  but if i look at my greek  again motivated to learn about greek  history about you know discovering that  that large part of the history of the  world when greeks were dominant from  about  uh you know at least in the eastern  mediterranean world from al even you  know from  pericles the building of the acropolis  and alexander the great the whole  biblical period when greek was the  dominant language  constantinople or at least emperor  constantine moved the roman empire  capital to constantinople then we had  byzantium byzantium which in turn  influenced the religions and much of the  art and so forth of the eastern of  eastern europe and russia and so forth i  mean it's a major major chunk of of uh  certainly european history and so i was  motivated to to read out read up about  that in greek and when i now can read  something that was written in ancient  greek and i at least can you know make  out the sound even though the language  is different all of that is tremendously  motivating  but  and it's compelling however i'm also  capable of listening to these mini  stories which are repetitive they use a  lot of the most common vocabulary the  vocabulary repeats in each story and and  i can tell from my statistics at link  that i have listened to these stories  you know my statistics say 50 times but  i listen on my mp3 player without it  showing up in my statistics so it's got  to be a hundred times each story  i don't listen to them a hundred times  at a shot i listen to it once i go  listen to something else maybe other  stories maybe i read about the acropolis  or listen to my podcast then i come back  to these many stories  and i'm able to do that because it is  it is relatively easy to do it's  compelling it's repetitive repetitive  the person's voice is pleasing  and it's it's um  you know it's like like training for  judo or or  for whatever it's this repetitive  exercise it's like going to the gym to  train  it's not the game the game is listening  to something that's interesting but it's  repetitive and every time i listen i  discover something that i missed the  previous time  and and i think actually even in a  class situation you know kids will do  what they're assigned to do so they will  listen to these simple stories  uh  to some extent and and to me it's a  little bit like the sort of uh  call it you know like the taoist  traditions of of chinese  you know literature or philosophy  uh  and which we can see in to some extent  in how like some of the you know the  martial arts how this constant  repetition of basic forms and and  patterns are repeated and repeated and  repeated until it becomes second nature  and it reminds me if i remember  correctly the story drunks are told of  this butcher who was butchering an ox  for the duke of when or whatever it  might be  and he explained that he just been doing  it for so long that that the first year  it was more difficult and by the third  year he no longer saw  the whole ox he saw where the tendons  and where where the bones were and and  because he had such a natural feel to  how of how to butcher the ox therefore  he didn't need to force it didn't he  need to force the knife and it's this  idea that we learn things through  getting used to them  through repetition  not through some theoretical explanation  and i think the the sort of repetitive  listening and particularly listening as  well as reading  uh more simple material blended in with  the more compelling content is an  important part of language learning and  now how much time i spend on  compelling content how much i spend on  this sort of repetitive content it's  more a matter of of my mood of the  moment and i think that that again is  important that we be guided by  our interest because we have to enjoy  the process so we i've you know  it is easy to listen to the simple  stories it's easier to listen to simple  stories when i'm exercising for example  in the car  i'm happier listening to interesting  content because i'm able to focus a  little better if i'm doing push-ups  it's better just to have these  repetitive stories going in the  background  but again it's something that we need to  decide for ourselves  uh  so i just want to make the point and and  and when i say repetitive it doesn't  mean that you should listen to the same  story over and over and over again i get  questions often like should i stay on a  bit of content until i have it 100  no  move on  and you should look forward to when you  can come back to that simple story and  the next time you'll pick up something  that you didn't get the previous time so  there will still be words that you may  have learned and subsequently forgotten  uh there'll be aspects of the grammar  that you may have noticed and  subsequently forgotten and you go back  and you listen to the same thing for the  80th time and you always pick up  something new so i think a regime of a  mixture of repetitive listening uh with  the compelling  compelling content is important and it  should be interleaved it's a term i've  used before  but it certainly has its role and it  does tie in and the reason i raise it  again is because it does tie into this  this sense of of learning through  through getting used to something which  is very much a part of of the sort of  zen and taoist  tradition of  of eastern philosophy so just wanted to  pass that on uh i hope that's uh  useful and i look forward to your  comments and if you like these videos  please subscribe bye for now  [Music]  you
 

